WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:06.750
RAMACHANDRAN IYER: Welcome.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.520
Hi, I'm Ram.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.692
I'm part of the marketplace
platforms at Uber.

00:00:11.692 --> 00:00:13.400
NANDAKUMAR GOPALAKRISHNAN:
Hi, I'm Nanda.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.258
I do work in marketplace
platforms at Uber.

00:00:16.258 --> 00:00:18.050
RAMACHANDRAN IYER:
Here's our agenda today.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:21.200
We'll start with an
overview of Uber scale

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.810
and some of the key challenges
we were looking to solve.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.180
We'll then look at what
it took us to evaluate

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.430
Cloud Spanner and
the systems we had

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:31.486
to build to enable our
platform on Spanner.

00:00:31.486 --> 00:00:34.970
We will then deep dive into
some technical learnings

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.040
and what we plan to do next.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.950
Let's look at what
scale means at Uber.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.240
Uber started back in
2010, and our systems

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.690
have constantly needed to
scale as business has evolved.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.720
As an engineer, one of
the fun things at Uber

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.830
is the opportunity to
build systems at scale.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.630
Here are some of
the numbers, based

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:59.810
on Q1, which essentially
highlights what's

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.030
the scale of our operations.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.190
We did close to
18 million rides,

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.040
which includes trips
and deliveries,

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.410
each day on the Uber platform.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:13.200
We had close to 5 million active
drivers and delivery people

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.400
and have close to 103 million
monthly active platform

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.690
consumers.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.500
Uber is a global company with
a presence in 10,000 cities

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.780
and 69 countries
across six continents.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.610
From the Eats side, we have
400,000 restaurant partners

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.840
operating globally
in 45 countries.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.180
A scale like this
creates a unique set

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.400
of technical challenges.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.730
The marketplace platform
team is responsible

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.800
for the fulfillment
platform which

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.110
facilitates the
core transportation

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.050
flows for Rides and Eats.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.410
We are huge into microservices.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.140
We have a robust
infrastructure running

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.740
on thousands of nodes across
hundreds of microservices,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.340
and the complexity in running
so many services increases

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.540
as we scale.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:09.979
Let's look at a simple example
of an offer acceptance flow.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:13.550
To your left, we
can see a snapshot

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:18.490
of the offer screen presented
to a driver for a Rides trip.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:22.230
The driver has an option
to accept or decline.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.880
And from the driver's
point of view,

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.710
it's just tap of a button,
but a lot of things

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.770
happen in the back.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.810
These drivers and
riders are modeled

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.120
as entities in our system.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.750
And when state changes
occur, all these entities

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.690
need to be updated
transactionally.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:42.200
As an example, once the
driver accepts the offer,

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.320
the rider also needs to be
associated with that driver,

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.270
and all the downstream
systems need

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.940
to be updated and kept in sync.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:54.710
Also, the presented offer needs
to auto-expire if no action has

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.710
been taken after a few seconds.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.340
And the feedback
for this also needs

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.690
to be propagated in
downstream systems.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:04.170
These entities are more
or less state machines

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:08.040
as it undergoes various entity
state transitions from start

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.310
to finish of a trip.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.050
Now, this is a
very simple example

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:16.100
of a two-sided marketplace
involving a driver and a rider.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.950
The complexity
increases with Eats,

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.680
which is a three-sided
marketplace involving

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.680
a restaurant, a delivery
person, and an eater,

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.930
also, with some
advanced use cases,

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:31.560
like trying to batch more trips
together for products like Uber

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.610
Pool and Eats deliveries.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.055
The fulfillment platform handles
all these real-time use cases.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.240
So more than a year back,
we took a holistic look

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.170
at our architecture with an aim
to simplify and modernize them.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.040
Let's look at how we approached.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.390
We categorized our challenges
into the following dimensions.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:55.730
One was focused on
platform efficiency

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:58.350
and the other on
data management.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:02.880
On platform efficiency, a lot
of product teams across Uber

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.870
used the fulfillment platform
to build business features.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.750
We have to ensure that we
build extensible components

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:14.250
across all our product lines
without introducing complexity.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.019
At the same time,
we want to make

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:19.920
sure we maximize on our
infrastructure efficiency

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.500
by improving
throughput per Core.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.600
Given the scale of Uber, even
an incremental improvement

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.900
here can have a
meaningful impact

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.640
on the overall
infrastructure spent.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.080
From data management
point of view,

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.410
our current application
architecture

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.950
relies on an eventually
consistent model

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.440
and adds complexity in
the application layer

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.830
to deal with consistency.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:47.770
Also, when you have
so microservices,

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.990
state management across all
of them is a huge challenge.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:55.260
For our systems to be reliable,
we need a strongly consistent

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.770
and an available database
that can scale to our needs

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.990
and can also support complex
transactions at scale.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:04.970
To overcome these
constraints, we

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:07.280
use solutions at
application level,

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.240
like maintaining transaction
queues, sticky sharding,

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:14.330
along with leveraging ring
flop for coordination,

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:18.760
and some patterns around
transaction orchestration.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.360
But if you think about it, this
application-level complexity

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.180
can be simplified by
pushing some of them

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:26.287
to the database load.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:32.360
So we classified our
database requirements further

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.800
into system and
usability aspects.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.790
From systems requirement, we
wanted an ACID-compliant system

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:42.550
which can give us fine
lines of availability

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:46.510
and comes with globally
consistent guarantees.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:50.150
Uber has multiple data
centers, so the solution

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.540
should work across
data centers and should

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:56.030
satisfy our latency issues.

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:59.030
From a usability standpoint,
we wanted support

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.620
for relational modeling.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.050
Based on the
previous example, we

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.900
wanted support for a
multifaceted marketplace

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:09.220
where business entities
are highly relational.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:10.990
It should have
good absorbability

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:13.390
for tracking and tracing
issues, and should

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:16.780
be easy to scale as
the business grows.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:18.640
So with these
requirements, which

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.280
are super high level
in mind, the team

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.860
looked at different
database solutions.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:26.020
We chose Cloud
Spanner for evaluation

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:28.410
as it satisfied all of
our core requirements.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:34.970
As we started ideating our
solution on top of Spanner,

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.380
we went through a
process of identifying

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:40.110
the gaps of what we
needed to build before we

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.780
could start using Spanner.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:44.310
Here are some of
the things which we

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:47.900
built specific to our use case.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:51.390
Our programming model
is highly asynchronous,

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:55.750
and we wanted all our Spanner
RPCs to be asynchronous, too.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:58.990
For this, we built an
async Spanner client

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:03.290
so that it can fit into
our execution model--

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:06.200
a Background Task
Execution system.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.590
We built an event-sourcing
solution on top of Spanner

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:13.910
which we will look into
greater detail further down.

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:16.990
TTL-- as part of a
real-time platform,

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.330
we needed support for TTL.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:22.990
So once a trip ends or
an order has fulfilled,

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:27.230
the data gets handled by a
post-trip processing platform.

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:30.350
TTL support is needed so
the data can be cleaned up

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:34.790
once it's no longer needed
by the real-time platform.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:37.050
A transaction
execution framework.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:39.060
We optimized on
different strategies

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.550
for executing our
transactions and also built

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:46.170
tooling around detecting
log contentions and ENDLOG.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:49.660
Lastly, for monitoring,
since all our metrics

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.770
are persisted in our internal
systems, some of which

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.890
are also open source,
like Entry and Jaeger,

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.020
we wanted Spanner's
server-side metrics

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:01.530
also to be available internally
and closely-integrated

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:04.360
with our monitoring
and alerting systems.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:08.240
Let's look at some
of them more closely.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.210
We started off with a very
lightweight Spanner client

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:15.200
generated using Google's
open source API generator

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:17.460
and built on top of the RPC.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.250
This is a Java client.

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.230
And as mentioned
previously, this

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:27.250
aligns closely and well with
our async programming pattern.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:29.510
It also gives us a
better handle for tuning

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:31.520
client-side resources.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.590
We added more
functionality to the client

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:37.049
as part of session and
transaction management.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.830
To give some more
context, a session

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:43.039
acts as a communication
channel, and you can only

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:46.250
run one transaction at
a time on a session.

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.490
They are reusable resources,
so we build session pooling

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:53.750
from ground up which can
auto scale based on usage.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:56.540
For transactions, we built
different transaction execution

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:59.540
strategies to reduce
our transaction time.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.360
More on this later
with an example.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.440
On observability front, we
wanted to integrate it closely

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:09.020
with our internal
observability platforms,

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:12.560
like logging,
metrics, and tracing.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:16.110
To talk more on other systems
and solutions we built,

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:20.580
I will hand over to Nanda,
who will take it from here.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:22.220
NANDAKUMAR GOPALAKRISHNAN:
Thanks, Ram.

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:24.990
To give you some context,
we wanted a system

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.210
for executing
background async tasks

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.150
with at least one guarantee.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:31.500
We were looking for an
event-sourcing solution

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.300
on top of Spanner.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:36.360
Such a solution would be used
for things like publishing

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.090
internal state change
events or to kick off

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.990
post-trip processing, like
sending customer notifications

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:44.550
for things like
ratings, et cetera.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.610
These tasks would be registered
as part of the same transaction

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:49.570
as for other entities.

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:52.530
For example, the driver
has ended the trip.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.480
The task to do
post-trip processing

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.280
would also be
registered as part of

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:59.010
the same end-trip transaction.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.440
Moreover, these
tasks also needed

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.620
to have a timer property
where they can be scheduled

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:06.900
to execute at
sometime in future,

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:10.740
or they can be scheduled to
execute tasks periodically.

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:13.620
To build such a system,
we introduced a task table

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:17.010
where we can store all
the registered tasks.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:21.210
We then built an efficient
scanner, also known as Tailers,

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:24.240
that scans through this
table and schedules them

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.306
in memory for execution.

00:10:26.306 --> 00:10:28.410
They were named as
Tailers inspired

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:33.180
by the Unix terminology, as the
scans only look for new data.

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:35.640
We were able to horizontally
scale the system

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.610
by having multiple tailers
at those different shards

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:41.970
so that each scan got
its own key space.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:43.860
This approach
allowed us to detect

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.200
any new tasks for
background execution

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.048
within milliseconds of
them being registered.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:55.910
Another system that we built was
a Spanner Monitoring service,

00:10:55.910 --> 00:10:58.220
which is a standalone
monitoring service that

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:00.500
inputs critical
Stackdriver metrics

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:03.610
into our internal
monitoring stack.

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:05.920
The reason why we did--

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:08.470
why we built this, was to
have Spanner Monitoring

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.520
well-integrated with our
internal systems, especially

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:13.980
dating and metric analysis.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:17.250
Some of the analysis that we
were able to perform well,

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:20.580
session analysis, that is to
check if sessions are properly

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:23.010
reused and for how long.

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:25.530
Then transaction
analysis, to analyze

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:27.370
long-running transactions.

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:29.940
That is Spanner provides
interesting stats which

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:34.350
allows us to introspect and dive
deeper into queries and BMLs

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:35.900
taking longer time.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:38.190
To get this data from
Spanner and index

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:42.910
it internally, making it
easier to search and analyze.

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:46.350
Building a Spanner monitoring
service allowed us to build--

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:49.090
also allowed us to build
an autoscaler functionality

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:50.710
recently.

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.870
To scale Spanner
nodes in an elastic,

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:55.150
were based on various
server side parameters

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:58.075
like Spanner CPU
Utilization and Storage.

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:03.430
While building these systems
and testing Spanner at scale,

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:05.260
we came across some
learnings that we

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:07.900
would like to share that
helped us along the way.

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:11.540
Let's dive deeper technically.

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:14.310
The first thing is
request patterns.

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:16.640
So it's important to have
good understanding of request

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:19.430
patterns in order to come
up with the right schema.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.920
It is also equally
important to understand

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.850
how Spanner performs reads
and writes for such a pattern.

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:28.300
For us, we had three
types of patterns,

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:31.670
simple CRUD-like operations
on a given group.

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:35.090
For such cases, completely
random primary key,

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:38.040
like UUID works really great.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.160
It gives us the best
possible distribution

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:42.830
and also avoids hot spots.

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:46.610
For multi-year updates or
for accessing related items,

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:48.230
use interleaved-tables.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:51.320
It gives locality without
[? intros, ?] ensuring

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:53.360
your joints are efficient.

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.600
The other use case,
as mentioned before,

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:57.310
was to build an
efficient scanner that

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:59.500
scans through new rows.

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:01.570
Frequent row deletions
in your table

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:05.410
can cause tombstones, which can
make your scans inefficient,

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:07.830
causing higher scan latencies.

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:09.850
For such use cases,
a combination

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:13.860
of shardId and Spanner
Timestamp works really well.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:16.020
The problem with
timestamps in a key,

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:19.790
either primary or secondary,
is that it can cause hot spots.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:22.920
Using shardId prevents the hot
spots and at the same time,

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.880
allows multiple workers
to scan individual shards

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:26.858
and [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:31.610
There were quite a
few learnings for us

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:35.260
with respect to how we do
transactions on Spanner.

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:37.940
So the first one was to
optimize for reducing

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:39.860
read/write transaction time.

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:41.900
Read/write transaction
time is measured

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:45.920
as the time between the first
operation in a transaction

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:48.700
to the final operation,
which is often the commit.

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:52.130
In general, increase in
read/write transaction time

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:55.070
is equal to increase
in lock contention

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:58.482
and consequently, increase
in abort rate in the system.

00:13:58.482 --> 00:14:01.710
When we started out, we had
a lot of aborted transactions

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.810
because of lock contentions.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:07.010
To have deeper understanding,
we built a transaction analyzer

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:09.300
to identify overlapping
transactions

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:11.970
and dig deeper into
each contention,

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:13.890
whether it is systemic
or whether it's

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:16.890
because of poor request
pattern, et cetera.

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:19.350
This allowed us to identify
the type of transactions

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:23.310
that can be read/write on abort
and the ones that should not.

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:25.560
This was important, as
the last thing you want

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:28.930
is all contending transactions
retrying forever, causing

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:31.820
mode churn and contentions.

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:34.360
For read-only
transactions, use snapshot

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:37.360
reads wherever possible to
increase your read performance.

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:40.810
This allows the reads to
be served from any replicas

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:45.890
and consequently, reduce
the load on the reader.

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:48.350
Let's look at an optimization
story with respect

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.180
to transaction execution.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:53.660
In the first graph, you
can see we started off

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:57.780
with a basic read, modified
write example where we read,

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:01.060
then write, and finally commit.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:04.040
In all, there were three
round trips to Spanner,

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:06.330
not including the
initial begin transaction

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:09.660
as we have a pool of
transactions ready to go.

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:12.660
Over time, our flows got
more complex where we often

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.770
had to do multiple reads and
writes within a transaction,

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:19.770
thus increasing the number
of round trips to Spanner.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:23.640
Now, why does round
trip to Spanner matter?

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:26.620
Depending on where
your reader center is,

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:29.670
each Spanner round trip has
a regional network latency.

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:33.090
For example, if a transaction
has five round trips,

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:35.190
and if network latency
is 30 milliseconds,

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:39.210
you have lost 150 milliseconds
just to the network.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:42.180
As we highlighted
earlier, keeping

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:44.310
the transaction
open for longer also

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:47.130
increases the chances
of contention.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:49.440
So to solve for this,
we systematically

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:51.860
broke down the
transaction by identifying

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:54.450
the parts of transaction that
can happen in [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:54.450 --> 00:15:57.240
by building a TransactionGraph.

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:00.270
This allowed us to issue
reads in [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:03.450
But writes were still
serialized as the BMLs needed

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.220
to be in sequence.

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:07.050
As illustrated in
graph three, this

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:10.235
allowed us to reduce the
round trips to Spanner

00:16:10.235 --> 00:16:12.180
by just finalizing reads.

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:16.950
To solve for writes, we matched
all writes and commit together

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:20.250
in a single RTC using the
mutations API as provided

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:22.490
by Spanner.

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:24.570
To look at other
learnings with respect

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:28.040
to Spanner client-side
and service-side tunings,

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:31.695
I will hand it over to Ram.

00:16:31.695 --> 00:16:33.070
RAMACHANDRAN IYER:
Thanks, Nanda.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:34.690
So here are some of
the considerations

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:37.660
for client-side
performance and tuning.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:41.060
We'll look at two main aspects
to tune on the client side--

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:43.300
sessions and gRPC.

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:46.240
We spoke about sessions
and session pool.

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:48.580
As part of session
pool configurations,

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:50.740
we recommend to
maintain or always

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:53.320
maintain necessary ratio
of read/write sessions

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:57.300
with the sessions that
are used only for reads.

00:16:57.300 --> 00:16:59.150
A read/write
session is a session

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:00.920
associated with a
transaction using

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:03.710
the begin transaction API.

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:08.210
You can size your session pool
based on the expected workload

00:17:08.210 --> 00:17:11.180
and transaction rate.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:14.770
Also, session management APIs
that create sessions and batch

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:17.609
create sessions
have high latency.

00:17:17.609 --> 00:17:19.460
It's better to
pre-provision sessions

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:24.680
instead of incurring costs
during transaction operations.

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:27.980
On the gRPC front,
we always need

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:33.250
to ensure to set appropriate
deadlines to the gRPC context.

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:35.020
We also learned
that comments should

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:40.170
be given more time to finish
compared to other operations.

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:43.030
For flow control,
gRPC streams are

00:17:43.030 --> 00:17:47.810
bi-directional streams and
are mapped to HTTP/2 streams.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:50.960
Payloads are serialized as
byte streams, which are then

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:54.270
fragmented into HTTP/2 frames.

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:58.970
gRPC uses flow control
mechanism in HTTP/2.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:03.410
And this also helps in finer
grain control for buffering

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:05.840
in-flight messages.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:07.700
We optimized our
flow control window

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:09.680
and depending on our
payload size, which

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:10.805
we found during our tests.

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:16.360
Compression-- this needs
to be carefully chosen

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:17.980
based on the payload size.

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:21.910
We found our product payloads
are highly compressible,

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:24.010
leading to bandwidth savings.

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:28.020
Currently, only Gs are supported
as part of the gRPC compression

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:29.350
codec.

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:34.030
We recommend on leveraging gRPC
interceptors for availability.

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:36.970
This can be plugged into
the client for introspection

00:18:36.970 --> 00:18:40.050
and tracing.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:44.220
From a server-side tuning point
of view, based on our testing,

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.720
we could scale
Spanner horizontally.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:49.190
One of the core metrics
for scaling Spanner nodes

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:51.510
is CPU usage.

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:56.190
It's recommended to operate
below 75% on a smooth CPU

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.200
usage.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:01.060
Spanner operational
management is simple,

00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:05.220
and it's easy to provision more
nodes as and when you need it.

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:08.040
It's also important to
choose appropriate regions,

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:12.890
as Spanner offers a variety
of options like nam3, nam6,

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:16.060
and regional Spanner instances.

00:19:16.060 --> 00:19:19.630
On the network side, since
we run our infrastructure

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:22.150
in our own data center,
it's important to have

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:26.680
an optimized routing
strategy to Spanner's server.

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:31.010
We work with GCP to
make this happen.

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:33.560
On the database
workload, Spanner

00:19:33.560 --> 00:19:38.030
performs database splits and
compactions based on traffic.

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:41.790
This can be a load or
a size-based split,

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:44.730
which might have a
brief impact on latency.

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:48.030
It's important to have an
appropriate ramp-up strategy

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:50.640
so that there is minimal
disruptions during the split.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:55.660
With all these learnings
and optimizations,

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:58.470
and along with the support
of the systems we built,

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.320
we performed
simulations on Spanner

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:04.150
with a synthetic
Uber scale load.

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:06.070
Before we look at
these numbers, it's

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:08.170
important to understand
these are strictly

00:20:08.170 --> 00:20:12.430
dependent on data modeling
and how transactions are run.

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:15.130
This is highly use-case
specific and might not

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:17.870
apply to every use case.

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:20.040
Here are the
following highlights.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.850
We were able to scale
Spanner nodes horizontally

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:28.980
in more than 600 nodes,
with a 330,000 per second--

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:34.530
reads per second and
130,000 writes per second.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:37.950
CPU usage stayed close to 67%.

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:40.050
And in terms of
latency, we mainly

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:42.390
cared about the following RPCs--

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:45.780
ExecuteSQL, execute
BatchDML, and commit

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:48.870
latency, which were within
our acceptable latency

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:51.130
requirements.

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:55.725
We were also working on a
bunch of more optimizations

00:20:55.725 --> 00:20:59.700
to better the performance.

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:02.273
So what are we doing next?

00:21:02.273 --> 00:21:03.690
We're looking
towards building out

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:07.610
new features like forecasting
our Spanner nodes based

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:13.200
on predicted load and
optimize on CPU usage.

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:16.460
We are also looking forward to
testing a few Spanner features,

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:19.880
like Backup and Restore.

00:21:19.880 --> 00:21:24.820
We'll also look at open sourcing
some of our work in the future.

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:27.330
Lastly, as we are working
on productionizing

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:32.190
our platform on a Cloud Spanner,
there's a whole team behind us.

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:34.680
And lots of people are
involved both on Uber

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:36.950
and from Google Cloud team.

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:39.650
As we embark on this journey,
we are looking forward

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:43.850
to this continued
collaboration and partnership.

00:21:43.850 --> 00:21:48.280
On behalf of Nanda, I thank
you for watching the talk.

